{"conversation_id":"aec61af3-a982-49c7-97f0-1b2199f90592","status":"SUCCESS","response":"### Root Cause\n\nIn [`pkg/cache.py`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L4-L5), `_key(spec)` generated the cache key using `tuple(spec.items())`:\n\n```python\ndef _key(spec):\n    return tuple(spec.items())\n```\n\nIn Python, dictionary `.items()` preserves insertion order. When two specification dictionaries have identical key-value pairs but different insertion orders (such as `{\"region\": \"eu\", \"tier\": \"gold\"}` vs `{\"tier\": \"gold\", \"region\": \"eu\"}` in `test_repeated_lookup`), `tuple(spec.items())` produced different tuples (`(('region', 'eu'), ('tier', 'gold'))` vs `(('tier', 'gold'), ('region', 'eu'))`), resulting in a cache miss.\n\n### Fix\n\nUpdated [`_key`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L4-L5) in [`pkg/cache.py`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py) to sort the items before constructing the tuple:\n\n```python\ndef _key(spec):\n    return tuple(sorted(spec.items()))\n```\n\n### Verification\n\nRan the test suite with `python -m pytest test_cache.py test_smoke.py -q`:\n- `test_cache.py`: 2 passed (both `test_basic_roundtrip` and `test_repeated_lookup`)\n- `test_smoke.py`: 2 passed\n- No test files were modified.\n","duration_seconds":136.8358648,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":286280,"output_tokens":8950,"thinking_tokens":5657,"cache_read_tokens":739928,"total_tokens":295230}}
